;Match details

* '''Match 1: Taumafa def. Tanaka.''' The giant's business-like win. Tanaka's brawling took the match to the floor; Taumafa's power ended it back in the ring. The Samoan driver at 11:14. Taumafa is 6-1, 12 points — tied with KAZUKI at the top of the block, and the Tsuda collision with Takagi now one show away. Tanaka falls to 1-6. '''Crowd:''' '''TAUMAFA''' chants. The Bulldog got the respect applause; the betrayal is a year old, and Anjo-Saikawa is a Seigigun city.

* '''Match 2: Kuroki def. Ono.''' The Kuroshio enforcer against the young ace. Ono's speed had Kuroki turning through the opening minutes; the Black Iron lariat at 13:19 was the answer. Kuroki is 4-3, 8 points, back in the middle after the KAZUKI loss; Ono falls to 3-4. '''Crowd:''' '''KUROKI''' chants, '''ONO''' calls behind them. The crowd has adopted the youngest man in the field all tour; the passing of the guard waits another year.

* '''Match 3: Jusuf def. Diallo.''' The truth-teller against the Hammer — the submission artist against the wall. Diallo's traditional Asikyran wrestling controlled the first half; Jusuf's counter was the kneebar, sunk in at 14:29. Jusuf is 5-2, 10 points — the block's quiet third, still alive in the math. Diallo falls to 2-5. '''Crowd:''' BOOS for Jusuf, louder than Tosa. The truth-teller walked out without acknowledging the applause for Diallo.

* '''Match 4: Ishida def. KAZUKI.''' The main event — and the block flipping upside down. KAZUKI, 6-1 and two years unbeaten in G1 block play before Tosa, opened with the full Shooting Star arsenal; Ishida, written off at 3-3, absorbed it and went to work on the leg. The Ishida Lock at 17:22 — the same hold that submitted Diallo on Day 4 and Takagi on Day 8 — made the man who ended the 1,095-day reign tap for the first time in two years. Clean submission. Ishida is 4-3, 8 points, level with Kuroki in the chasers' tier; KAZUKI falls to 6-2, and the block's leadership is now a tie with the head-to-heads scrambled. '''Crowd:''' '''ISHIDA''' chants, stunned into the loudest pop of the night at the tap. STANDING OVATION for the veteran. KAZUKI sat in the corner for a long moment before standing and bowing — the rival's respect, given to the man nobody saw coming.

* '''Match 5: Takagi def. Reyes.''' The Dragon's hold serve. Reyes, 0-6, fought like a man refusing a shutout for the second straight show; the Dragon lariat at 9:58 was the block's most decisive single move doing what it does. Takagi is 5-2, 10 points; Reyes falls to 0-7. '''Crowd:''' '''TAKAGI''' chants. GASPS at the lariat. Respect applause for Reyes, who stood and bowed again.

